  "worked_solution": "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}\\ge 4)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given inequality is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{4} \\\\ |x| & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}\\ge 4)\\,⇒\\,({x}^{2}\\ge 1)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEvery solution of ${x}^{2}\\ge 4$ satisfies ${x}^{2}\\ge 1.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}\\ge 4)\\,⇏\\,({x}^{2}\\ge 7)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=2.5,$ then ${x}^{2}=6.25\\ge 4,$ but ${x}^{2}≱7.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}\\ge 4)\\,⇏\\,(x=2)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=3,$ then ${x}^{2}=9,$ but $x\\ne 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
